{"question_id": "Medical-b7f57756", "method": "lightrag", "answer": "Based on the context, less common cancer subtypes in bladder cancer include adenocarcinoma and small cell carcinoma.", "contexts": ["pathologist. The pathologist will compare the cancer cells to normal cells. The more different they look, the higher the grade. And the higher the grade, the faster the cancer is expected to spread. LG means that the cancer cells are low grade (slow growing). HG means that the cancer cells are high grade (fast growing). The inner lining of the bladder is made of urothelial cells. Most cases of bladder cancer start in the urothelial cells. So this type of cancer is called urothelial cancer. But in rare cases, urothelial cell cancer has features that look like other types of cells. These are referred to as subtypes. Some of them include: Glandular subtype Squamous cell subtype Micropapillary subtype Plasmacytoid subtype Sarcomatoid subtype Nested subtype Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype Microcystic and tubular subtype It’s important to investigate whether you have one of these or another subtype. In general, treatment for these uncommon subtypes is similar in the early stages to treatment for typical urothelial cancer. However, some subtypes might grow or advance faster than typical urothelial cell cancer. And faster growth might mean it needs more aggressive treatment. Key points An important thing about bladder cancer is whether it has or it hasn’t grown into the muscle layer. Cancer that has invaded the muscle needs more aggressive treatment. The stage of cancer describes how deep it has grown into the bladder wall and how far it has spread. Cancer stage is used to determine which tests and treatments will help you receive the best outcome. Bladder cancer at stage 0 and stage 1 haven’t invaded the muscle layer. Stage 0 means that abnormal cells have grown on the inside lining of the bladder. Stage 1 means that a tumor has formed and has invaded the layer of connective tissue in the bladder wall. Stages 2, 3, and 4 bladder cancers are muscle-invasive. The tumor has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall. In stage 3 bladder cancer, the tumor has grown into the fat layer around the bladder. There may also be cancer in nearby lymph nodes. Stage 4 is metastatic bladder cancer. This is cancer that has spread outside the bladder and to distant sites in the body. Cancer grade is an estimate of how fast the cancer is expected to grow. Low grade bladder cancer is slow growing. High grade cancer grows more quickly. We want your feedback! Our goal is to provide helpful and easy-to-understand information on cancer. Take our survey to let us know what we got right and what we could do better. NCCN.org/patients/feedback Treatment options for bladder cancer Surgery Intravesical therapy Systemic therapy Radiation therapy Clinical trials Supportive care Side effects of treatment Key points What’s your stage? This chapter explains the different treatment options for bladder cancer. Many people with bladder cancer receive more than one type of treatment. Together, you and your care team will choose a treatment plan that’s right for you. Treatment for bladder cancer is based on many things. This includes the extent, severity, and type of cancer. It also includes your age, your ability to perform daily tasks, your other health issues, and the availability and affordability of drugs. Importantly, treatment is also based on your wishes and preferences. There are several ways to treat bladder cancer. It’s likely that you’ll need a few different types of treatment over months or years. Treatments include: Surgery – The goal of surgery is to take out the tumor and sometimes the surrounding tissue. Surgery procedures range from snipping a single tumor from the bladder lining to removing the entire bladder and other organs. Intravesical therapy – Intravesical therapy involves filling the bladder with a liquid medication that helps destroy cancer cells. Systemic therapy – Systemic therapy is typically an intravenous (IV) treatment that treats cancer anywhere in the whole body. Types of systemic therapy include chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy. Radiation therapy – Radiation therapy is a local therapy, which means it affects a specific area of the body. Sometimes chemotherapy or another therapy is added to radiation therapy to enhance treatment. Clinical trial – Clinical trials are done to test experimental treatments. You may be able to join a clinical trial at any time. Surgery Types of surgery for bladder cancer include tumor removal and bladder removal. Tumor removal For bladder cancer, a surgical procedure called a resection removes the tumor but leaves the rest of the bladder in place. The full name of the procedure is called a transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT, sometimes pronounced tur-bit). Even though it has a complicated name, the concept of TURBT is simple. It’s a procedure that removes tumors through the urethra without having to cut into the abdomen. The tumor can then be tested to provide a more detailed diagnosis. TURBT is done in an operating room in a clinic or hospital. You’ll be given anesthesia to put you to sleep. The urologist then guides an instrument with a small cutting device through the urethra and into the bladder. The instrument is used to examine and remove the tumor from the bladder lining. The goals of TURBT are to: Confirm the initial bladder cancer diagnosis. Take a sample of the bladder wall to see if the tumor has invaded the muscle layer. Remove all the visible tumor. After the procedure, most people can go home the same day. But you’ll need a few days to rest and recover. You may feel pain or burning when you urinate and have blood in your urine for the following 2 weeks or so. Another TURBT procedure may be needed about 2 to 6 weeks after the first one. The second TURBT is done to make sure all the tumor is removed. Or it may be done to get a deeper biopsy sample for the pathologist to examine. A treatment called intravesical therapy is often done in combination with TURBT."], "entities": [{"entity_name": "Mucinous Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Adenocarcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Adenocarcinoma is a broad category of cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are responsible for producing and secreting substances such as mucus. This cancer type can arise in various organs, and its characteristics and prevalence differ depending on the location. In the lungs, adenocarcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) that begins in the mucus-making cells lining the alveoli. It is the most common subtype of NSCLC. In the colorectal region, adenocarcinoma is the most frequent type of tumor, forming within adenomas (precancerous polyps) and representing the most common form of both colon and rectal cancer. It also accounts for the vast majority of colorectal cancers overall, distinguishing it from cancers arising from squamous cells.\n\nAdenocarcinoma also occurs in other parts of the digestive and reproductive systems. In the stomach, it is the most common type of cancer, developing from the mucus-secreting cells that line the stomach wall; indeed, nearly all stomach cancers are adenocarcinomas. In the cervix, adenocarcinoma is a recognized type of cervical cancer that arises from the glandular cells located in the endocervix, and it is considered a common form of this disease. Additionally, adenocarcinoma can appear in the bladder, where it is a less common, non-urothelial type of cancer. Across all these sites, the defining feature of adenocarcinoma is its origin in glandular tissue, which influences its behavior, diagnosis, and treatment.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682927}, {"entity_name": "Low-Grade Serous Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer with low-grade features.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the bladder, most commonly originating in the urothelial cells that line the inside of the organ. This abnormal cell growth can be classified into several clinical categories, including non-muscle-invasive, muscle-invasive, and metastatic forms, reflecting the extent to which the cancer has spread within or beyond the bladder wall. The disease is most frequently diagnosed in adults aged 55 and older, and its development is influenced by risk factors such as smoking and genetic predisposition.\n\nDiagnosis of bladder cancer typically involves a range of tests and procedures aimed at detecting the presence and extent of the malignancy. The condition is often identified through imaging, urine tests, and biopsy, with the specific diagnostic approach tailored to the individual case. Once diagnosed, the cancer is staged and graded to determine its aggressiveness and spread, which directly informs the treatment strategy.\n\nTreatment for bladder cancer is highly individualized, depending on the stage and grade of the tumor, the patient’s overall health, and their personal preferences. Options may include surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, immunotherapy, or a combination of these approaches. Given the complexity of the disease, management often requires a multidisciplinary care team, including urologists, oncologists, and other specialists, to coordinate comprehensive care. The goal of treatment is to remove or destroy the cancer while preserving bladder function whenever possible, though more advanced or recurrent cases may necessitate more aggressive interventions.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683092}, {"entity_name": "Malignant Germ Cell Tumors", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Less common non-epithelial ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Small Cell Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common type of bladder cancer.<SEP>A non-urothelial type of bladder cancer that is less common.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Clear Cell Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Tumor", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor is an abnormal mass or growth of tissue that arises from the uncontrolled overgrowth of cells, and it can be either benign (non-cancerous) or malignant (cancerous). In the context of cancer, a tumor specifically refers to the primary cancerous mass, which is a key element in staging and treatment planning. Tumors can develop in various parts of the body, including lymph nodes, the prostate, the bladder, bones, the throat, and the ovaries, where they may present as solid or fluid-filled masses. Their size is often measured in centimeters or millimeters, and this measurement, along with the extent of local invasion into nearby structures, is used to assign a T score in cancer staging systems. For example, primary bone tumors are staged based on size and location, while throat and bladder tumors are assessed for their potential to invade deeper tissue layers or spread. Tumors may be detected as irregular or hardened areas during physical examination, such as in the prostate, or identified through medical procedures. Biopsy samples are commonly taken from tumors to determine whether they are cancerous, and in many cases, tumors can be surgically removed. Overall, the term tumor encompasses a wide range of abnormal growths, with its clinical significance largely determined by its benign or malignant nature, size, location, and potential for invasion or metastasis.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788682948}, {"entity_name": "Low-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A less common epithelial ovarian cancer with low-grade features.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Kidney Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A disease characterized by malignant cells in the kidneys.<SEP>Cancer that starts in the tissues of the kidneys, with most being carcinomas.<SEP>Cancer that arises in the kidney, with renal cell carcinoma being the most common type.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683065}, {"entity_name": "RCC Subtypes", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Different subtypes of renal cell carcinoma, a type of kidney cancer.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683065}, {"entity_name": "Malignant Sex-Cord Stromal Tumors", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Less common non-epithelial ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683049}, {"entity_name": "Low Grade Bladder Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "Bladder cancer that is slow growing, indicated by LG.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "Inhibin", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor marker (typically inhibin A and B) found in higher amounts in less common ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683054}, {"entity_name": "Urothelial Cancer", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "The most common type of bladder cancer, arising from urothelial cells.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683089}, {"entity_name": "CA 19-9", "entity_type": "concept", "description": "A tumor marker that may be elevated in less common ovarian cancers.", "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683054}], "relations": [{"src_id": "Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "White Males", "description": "White males have a lower risk of prostate cancer compared to Black males.<SEP>White males have a lower risk of developing prostate cancer compared to Black males.", "keywords": "incidence,lower risk,risk", "weight": 2.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080<SEP>doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Adenocarcinoma", "tgt_id": "Bladder Cancer", "description": "Adenocarcinoma is a type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "classification,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Exercise", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Exercise may decrease the aggressiveness of prostate cancer and the likelihood of dying from it.", "keywords": "protective factor", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Small Cell Carcinoma", "description": "Small cell carcinoma is a type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "classification,type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Low-Risk Prostate Cancer", "tgt_id": "Prostate Cancer", "description": "Low-risk prostate cancer is a category indicating a lower chance of recurrence or spread.", "keywords": "prognosis,risk classification", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683103}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Tumor", "description": "Bladder cancer may form a tumor in the bladder.", "keywords": "pathology,progression", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Mucinous Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Mucinous carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Kidney Cancer", "tgt_id": "RCC Subtypes", "description": "RCC subtypes are different types of renal cell carcinoma, a form of kidney cancer.", "keywords": "classification,types", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683102}, {"src_id": "Low-Grade Serous Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Low-grade serous carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Low Grade Bladder Cancer", "description": "Low grade bladder cancer is a slow-growing form of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "grade", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Malignant Germ Cell Tumors", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Malignant germ cell tumors are a less common non-epithelial subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Bladder Cancer", "tgt_id": "Urothelial Cancer", "description": "Urothelial cancer is the most common type of bladder cancer.", "keywords": "type", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683105}, {"src_id": "Clear Cell Carcinoma", "tgt_id": "Ovarian Cancer", "description": "Clear cell carcinoma is a less common subtype of ovarian cancer.", "keywords": "classification,subtype", "weight": 1.0, "source_id": "doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040", "file_path": "unknown_source", "created_at": 1788683101}, {"src_id": "Breast Cancer", "tgt_id": "Carcinoma", "description": "Almost all breast cancers are a subtype of carcinoma.<SEP>Almost all breast cancers are carcinomas.", "keywords": "cancer,disease,subt